/UK, plus direct enterprise contracts.\nUnderstandability: High.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat (0\u20133 each)\n\u2022 Intangible assets: 2 (B2B brand modest; IP in localization stack)\n\u2022 Switching costs: 2 (deep integrations; multi-homing possible)\n\u2022 Network effects: 2 (merchant scale \u2192 better carrier/payment coverage)\n\u2022 Cost advantage: 2 (scale carrier/duty processing)\n\u2022 Economies of scale: 2\n\u2022 Regulatory barriers: 3 (duty/tax/data/compliance MoR complexity \u2014 highest bar)\n\u2022 Data moat: 2 (duty/shipping tables + AI cost-to-serve)\nTotal: 15/21 \u2192 **Wide moat.** Direction: **Stable-to-widening** (v2.0 adoption,\nPassport logistics, AI efficiency) with one narrowing vector: Shopify in-housing.\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch: **Fast Grower.** Checkpoints: GMV +44% [Actual], rev +39% [Actual],\nadj EBITDA margin +300bp [Actual] \u2014 growth + profitability both confirmed.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial strength scorecard (FY2025, [Actual] unless noted)\n\u2022 Revenue growth: 27.8% FY25; 5-yr CAGR 47.8%\n\u2022 GM: 45.3% (FY25); improving 28.8%\u219245.3% over 7 yrs\n\u2022 GAAP op margin: 7.5% (median 7-yr \u22129.0%)\n\u2022 Net margin: 7.1%; ROE: 17.0% [Aggregator]\n\u2022 FCF (OCF\u2212capex): $281M; FCF\u2212SBC: $242M\n\u2022 Net cash: liquidity $530M @6/30/26 [Actual] vs op leases $24M \u2192 ~$500M net cash\n\u2022 Piotroski 6/7; Accruals \u22120.158 (earnings cash-backed)\nJudgment: **Excellent.**\n\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard (/15)\n1. Buyback: 2 \u2014 $200M plan completed ($68M in Q2 2026 [Actual]); new $500M\n   authorization [Actual], but issuance for Passport ($~175M stock) offsets.\n2. M&A: 1 \u2014 Passport track record unproven; $3\u20134M H2 adj EBITDA on $350M.\n3. Capex: 3 \u2014 $3M on $962M revenue (0.3%); incremental ROIC very high.\n4. Mix rationality: 2 \u2014 balanced R&D/S&M/buyback/M&A; 30.5% insider alignment.\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3 \u2014 beat-and-raise every 2026 quarter [Actual].\nInsider bonus: +1 (30.5% insider ownership) [Aggregator]; no 6-mo insider net\nbuy/sell data [Unconfirmed].\nTotal \u2248 **12/16 \u2014 Good.**\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 2.5 \u2014 INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\n\u2022 SBC [Actual]: FY2025 $39M = 4.1% of revenue. Adjusted FCF = OCF \u2212 capex \u2212 SBC =\n  $284M \u2212 $3M \u2212 $39M = **$242M** [Arithmetic]. GAAP op income already expensed SBC,\n  so DCF below is SBC-clean by construction.\n\u2022 R&D [Estimate]: non-GAAP R&D \u2248 10\u201312% of revenue \u2192 FY2025 \u2248 $105M. Capitalizing\n  3-yr amortization lifts adjusted op income \u2248 +$65M and unamortized R&D into\n  invested capital \u2248 +$180M.\n\u2022 Adjusted ROIC [Estimate]: GAAP ROIC = NOPAT/IC = $61M/$508M \u2248 12%\n  (IC = equity $933M + leases $24M \u2212 cash&ST-inv $449M). R&D-capitalized ROIC \u2248 18%.\n  Gap >5pts \u2192 explains premium; economics are real but not GAAP-visible.\n\u2022 Owner earnings (FY2025) [Estimate] = NI $68M + D&A $2M \u2212 maint. capex $3M \u2212\n  non-float \u0394WC ~$15M \u2248 **$52M**.\n\u2022 Float caveat [Unconfirmed\u2192Estimate]: OCF $284M far exceeds NI $68M; \u0394current\n  liabilities +$162M (merchant payables/funds in transit) inflates OCF. True\n  sustainable FCF sits between NOPAT (~$61M) and headline FCF ($242M); my DCF uses\n  the conservative NOPAT-anchored definition.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (ENGINE 1 DCF + REVERSE DCF CROSS-CHECK)\n\nDiscount rate derivation (WACC = CoE; debt-free)\n\u2022 Rf = 4.70% [Actual \u2014 10Y UST 8/21/26]\n\u2022 ERP = 4.50% [Estimate \u2014 Damodaran-style implied, elevated-rate world]\n\u2022 Beta = 1.10 [Assumption \u2014 vendor beta 1.011 [Aggregator], small growth tilt]\n\u2022 Size premium = 0.30% [Assumption]; Israel country premium = 0.25% [Assumption]\n\u2022 CoE = 4.70 + 1.10\u00d74.50 + 0.30 + 0.25 = **10.20%**\n\nForecast path (every driver sourced):\nREVENUE \u2014 FY26 $1.330B = guidance midpoint [Actual, company]; FY27 +27.0% =\nconsensus 27.5% [Estimate, yfinance via pack]; FY28\u201335 +22/+19/+16/+14/+12/+10/+9/+8%\n[Assumption \u2014 deceleration to 17.2% 10-yr CAGR; TAM cross-border multi-trillion,\nGLBE <2% share; not extendable beyond guided yr without assumption].\nMARGIN (GAAP EBIT) \u2014 FY26 13.0% [Estimate \u2014 Q2 GAAP net margin 16% [Actual] vs\nFY25 7.5% [Actual]; SBC/D&A gap to adj-EBITDA 21.7% guide [Actual]; 13% chosen\nconservatively]; FY27\u201335 13.5\u219217.5% (+~0.5pt/yr) [Assumption]; terminal 17.5%\n[Assumption \u2014 implies adj EBITDA ~26\u201328%, near commerce-platform best-in-class].\nD&A \u2014 2.0%\u21921.0% of rev [Assumption \u2014 Passport intangibles amortization fading].\nCAPEX \u2014 0.40% of rev [Estimate \u2014 FY25 actual 0.31%].\n\u0394NWC \u2014 10% of incremental rev to FY31, 7.5% FY32, 5% FY33+ [Assumption \u2014\nfloat-adjusted; conservative vs 49.6% gross WC/revenue].\nTAX 15% [Assumption]; TERMINAL g 3.5% [Assumption, \u2264 nominal GDP ~4.5%];\nTERMINAL ROIC 20% [Assumption].\n\nBase-case DCF ($B):\nYear      Rev   EBIT%  NOPAT   D&A  Capex  \u0394NWC   FCF     DF@10.2%  PV\nFY26     1.330  13.0  0.147  0.027 0.005 0.037  0.132   0.907    0.119\nFY27     1.689  13.5  0.194  0.030 0.007 0.036  0.182   0.823    0.149\nFY28     2.061  14.0  0.245  0.031 0.008 0.037  0.231   0.747    0.172\nFY29     2.452  14.5  0.302  0.032 0.010 0.039  0.285   0.678    0.193\nFY30     2.845  15.0  0.363  0.028 0.011 0.039  0.341   0.615    0.210\nFY31     3.243  15.5  0.427  0.032 0.013 0.040  0.407   0.558    0.227\nFY32     3.632  16.0  0.494  0.036 0.015 0.029  0.487   0.507    0.247\nFY33     3.996  16.5  0.560  0.040 0.016 0.018  0.566   0.460    0.260\nFY34     4.355  17.0  0.629  0.044 0.017 0.018  0.638   0.417    0.266\nFY35     4.704  17.5  0.700  0.047 0.019 0.018  0.710   0.379    0.269\nSum PV FCF = 2.113\nTerminal: NOPAT36 0.724 \u00d7 (1 \u2212 3.5%/20%) = 0.597 \u2192 TV 0.597/0.067 = 8.918\nPV TV = 8.918 \u00d7 0.379 = 3.376\nEV = 2.113 + 3.376 = 5.489\n+ Net cash 0.500 (liquidity $530M [Actual] \u2212 op leases $24M [Actual]) \nEquity = 5.989 \u2192 /168.0M sh = **$35.65/share base IV** [Estimate]\n\nReverse DCF (Engine 4 cross-check): Market EV = 6.811 \u2212 0.500 = $6.311B, i.e.\n~15% above my base EV. The price embeds either ~19% 10-yr revenue CAGR at my\nmargins, or ~19.5\u201320% terminal GAAP margin at my growth, or ~9.4% WACC \u2014 i.e.,\n**near-bull execution is required at T0**.\n\nMultiples cross-check: EV/FY26E rev 4.7x; EV/FY27E rev 3.7x; EV/FY26E adj EBITDA\n21.8x; EV/(adj EBITDA \u2212 SBC) \u2248 27x; fwd GAAP P/E \u2248 42x [Estimate]; fwd non-GAAP\nP/E \u2248 27x. Not cheap, not distressed \u2014 quality growth is fully paid for.\n\nTornado (\u00b11\u03c3 on base $35.65):\n\u2022 Terminal GAAP margin \u00b13pts \u2192 $27.5 / $44.5 (drivers: SBC control, scale)\n\u2022 10-yr rev CAGR \u00b13pts (14.2%/20.2%) \u2192 $28.0 / $44.0 (drivers: Managed Markets, tariffs)\n\u2022 WACC \u00b11pt (9.2%/11.2%) \u2192 $41.5 / $30.5 (drivers: Fed path, 10Y)\nTop-3 KPIs = revenue growth, GAAP/SBC-diluted margin, 10Y yield. (These are the\nmonitoring hooks in Section 8.)\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\n\u2022 Competitive response: \u22122. If GLBE wins big, Shopify (its landlord) is the first\n  responder \u2014 it already owns checkout, payments, tax; internalizing cross-border\n  logistics is strategically rational. Also PayPal/Amazon and freight aggregators.\n\u2022 Reflexivity: +1. High stock price funds SBC-based talent acquisition and\n  share-based M&A (Passport paid partly in stock [Actual]); weak price would\n  threaten neither solvency (net cash) but would raise SBC dilution economics.\n\u2022 Customer evolution: +1. Early-adopter DTC brands \u2192 mainstream enterprise\n  (Ferrari, LVMH logos [Actual]); duty-drawback product converts tariff headwind\n  into a service [Actual].\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\nBayesian update: Prior P(sustain \u226515% CAGR a decade) \u2248 